01|tree-example|a-m3-p3-tree|examples/a-m3-p3-tree/main.rs|15|24||golden OK|p3_json|assert_eq|The example fits the tree through p3 json, then reads the golden file and asserts that our confusion matrix equals it exactly.|Cargo runs it live: accuracy near ninety eight percent, a confusion matrix with only two strays, and the contract line turns green.
02|kmeans-example|a-m3-p3-kmeans|examples/a-m3-p3-kmeans/main.rs|15|30||e-12 OK|p3_json|assert!|The k-means example reuses the same call, then asserts the objective is within one e minus six of golden and the cluster sizes match exactly.|Cargo runs it: objective three seventy eight, cluster sizes one thirty three, one twenty three, eighty six, and the green verdict closes the scene.
03|unit-tests|a-m3-p3-tree|tests/p3.rs|21|36|cargo test --locked --offline --test p3|test result: ok.|classifier_matches|kmeans_matches|Two Rust tests pin the classifier to the golden confusion matrix and k-means to the golden objective, with no tolerance on the integer counts.|Cargo test runs all eight tests live, and the result line turns green: eight passed, none failed.
04|gini-risk|a-m3-p3-tree|src/p3.rs|92|101||golden OK|majority|powi|Risk counts the rows outside the majority class, and Gini impurity is n times one minus the sum of squared class shares.|Cargo runs the tree example, which calls both on every node, and the contract line confirms the confusion matrix still equals golden.
05|best-split|a-m3-p3-tree|src/p3.rs|111|122||golden OK|MINBUCKET|gain|For each feature, sort the rows, slide a cut point along them, skip cuts that leave a child under seven rows, and score the gain.|Cargo runs the tree example, which scans every feature this way, and the green verdict shows the chosen splits reproduce the golden matrix.
06|grow|a-m3-p3-tree|src/p3.rs|128|142||golden OK|MINSPLIT|CP \*|Grow stops at a leaf when a node has fewer than twenty rows, is pure, or when the split saves less than one percent of the root risk.|Cargo runs the tree example built on this recursion, and the contract line turns green when the tree matches golden.
07|predict-one|a-m3-p3-tree|src/p3.rs|156|171||golden OK|Tree::Leaf|row\[|Prediction walks the tree: a leaf returns its class, and a split sends the row left when its feature is below the threshold.|Cargo runs the tree example, which predicts the test rows this way, and the verdict line turns green on an exact confusion match.
08|kmeans-standardize|a-m3-p3-kmeans|src/p3.rs|196|212||e-12 OK|std_axis|usize::MAX|K-means first standardizes the features with the mean and sample standard deviation, and starts every cluster label at the maximum value.|Cargo runs the k-means example on that standardized data, and the green verdict confirms the objective is within one e minus six of golden.
09|kmeans-converge|a-m3-p3-kmeans|src/p3.rs|214|229||e-12 OK|next == cluster|let objective|The loop stops when the labels stop changing, otherwise it moves each centroid to its cluster mean, and the objective sums squared distances.|Cargo runs the example, and the output shows the objective and the cluster sizes before the contract line turns green.
10|mutants|a-m3-p3-tree|tests/p3.rs|40|55|cargo test --locked --offline --test p3|test result: ok.|can_fail_with_other|assert_ne|Two mutant tests prove the gate can fail: other centroids must change the k-means result, and a stump tree must lose accuracy.|Cargo test runs the two mutant tests live, and both pass, which means the golden checks really do catch a broken model.
